{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-81273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}\\,\\,v \\\\ u+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}\\,\\,v$ and $y(u,v)=u+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={u}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2uv & {u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2uv\\cdot 2v-{u}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4u{v}^{2}-{u}^{2} \\\\  & =u(4{v}^{2}-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ u(4{v}^{2}-u) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equation $u(4{v}^{2}-u)=0$ defines a pair of intersecting curves in the $uv$-plane: the line $u=0$ and the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c86794cf22feead15f1b444d02ae7bf292d447e5e16cbac7495703637880471e"} {"question" "q-81273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=-v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v$ and $u=-v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}v(u-1)^{2} \\\\ v{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=v(u-1)^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=v{u}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2(u-1)v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =(u-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2(u-1)v & (u-1)^{2} \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u-1)v\\cdot {u}^{2}-(u-1)^{2}\\cdot 2uv \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}(u-1)v-2u(u-1)^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}(u-1)v-2u(u-1)^{2}v & =0 \\\\ 2u(u-1)v(u-(u-1)) & =0 \\\\ 2u(u-1)v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u(u-1)v=0$ defines the lines $u=0,$ $u=1,$ and $v=0$ in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c86794cf22feead15f1b444d02ae7bf292d447e5e16cbac7495703637880471e"} {"question" "q-81275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0,$ $u=1,$ and $v=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0,$ and $u=-v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0,$ and $v=u.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v$ and $v=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v,$ and $u=-v.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0,$ $u=1,$ and $v=0.$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+3{v}^{2} \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+3{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u-2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 6v \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2)-6v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u+3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u+3v=0$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c86794cf22feead15f1b444d02ae7bf292d447e5e16cbac7495703637880471e"} {"question" "q-81279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}=1.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$"] ["text" "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{2}{3}.$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-81279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x-2y \\\\ 3x+4y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=x-2y$ and $v(x,y)=3x+4y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c86794cf22feead15f1b444d02ae7bf292d447e5e16cbac7495703637880471e"} {"question" "q-160479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \nHere, we have $u(x,y)=xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{y} \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c86794cf22feead15f1b444d02ae7bf292d447e5e16cbac7495703637880471e"} {"question" "q-160599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2u"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2v}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{v}"] ["math" "2uv"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2u}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"} {"question" "q-160599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2v}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069450/state.json" "file_sha256" "e0f6c97148bd7610114b7d979d4440583dd08d941aad3b18cc4955b9c1bc48d0"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2378 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
